1.Additur and remittitur. Where the court determines that the jury made a mistake in awarding damages, the court has two options—order a retrial of the damages issues, or adjust the damages award itself without retrial. If the court augment’s the jury’s award, it is referred to as additur; a downward adjustment is remittitur. In some states, a court may only grant such a motion with the consent of both parties. See, e.g., Riddle v. Golden Isles Broadcasting, LLC, 292 Ga.App. 888, 666 S.E.2d 75 (2008). In the absence of agreement, the alternative is a new trial on damages. In many jurisdictions, a judge may grant additur or remittitur only where the jury’s award falls, “as a matter of law, outside the scope of the evidence,” or where it “shocks the conscience.” In other jurisdictions, the standard is more stringent, overturning the jury only where the court feels that the jury was influenced by “bias, prejudice, or corruption.” See, e.g., Rodgers v. Pascagoula Pub. Sch. Dist., 611 So.2d 942 (Miss.1992). Perhaps because the tests are so stringent, additur and remittitur are rarely used to resolve perceived problems of inequity between awards. David Baldus et al., Improving Judicial Oversight of Jury Damages Assessments: A Proposal for the Comparative Additur/Remittitur Review of Awards for Nonpecuniary Harms and Punitive Damages, 80 Iowa L. Rev. 1109 (1994–95).

